{
  "image_name": "ad10863285dab3461a6b356a8b1ab5fa.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure is a schematic cross-section of Earth’s mantle showing how deep mantle circulation may connect surface plate tectonics with hotspots and plume volcanism. The surface includes a mid-ocean ridge, subduction zones, and a hotspot that feeds hotspot tracks or large igneous provinces. Dashed arrows outline broad mantle convection, with cold material sinking at subduction zones and warmer material rising elsewhere.\n\nNear the core–mantle boundary, two large low-shear-velocity provinces (LLSVPs) sit above the core, with thin ultra-low-velocity zones (ULVZs) at their margins. Bright plume-like columns rise from these deep regions toward the surface, suggesting that hotspots may originate near the edges or tops of LLSVPs. One side emphasizes downwelling, where subducted slabs descend deep into the mantle.\n\nThe inset enlarges the base of one plume. It shows an ascending, chemically zoned mantle plume interacting with surrounding lower mantle and apparently entraining subducted material as it rises. The plume root seems to begin near the ULVZ and LLSVP above the core, highlighting a possible mixing zone between deep mantle reservoirs, recycled slab material, and plume source regions.",
